50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268706} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea4a91e-6bb0-52e0-a41d-3ada0c882377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268707} {:answer/id #uuid "9c361f45-990b-5b56-b047-55387ce85b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268708} {:answer/id #uuid "7c948b41-d298-504b-b832-ac7bb1fb50c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268709} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5f4a6f-7502-5f82-8658-59b212815d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ea4a91e-6bb0-52e0-a41d-3ada0c882377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268707} :answer-field/id #uuid "9016f124-6f8a-5f4d-8eae-208a665c53ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268705}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9985084e-755c-51fb-80a8-a70831820a0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117250" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(0,\\pi )$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,0)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2df217d3-d1d2-54cd-8c93-7925225bfdf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d797a3b7-b6da-5fb7-b1fa-a884fa9e6383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268713} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b8558e-342c-5d1a-b627-544691ad19f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268714} {:answer/id #uuid "af86d0cb-3ad3-5914-9a84-0ede4847267e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268715} {:answer/id #uuid "964c8968-f5b7-5a43-bdc4-cbf8b94cb914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268716} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8ce35f-af17-5e38-b346-c1dc25d386f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "964c8968-f5b7-5a43-bdc4-cbf8b94cb914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268716} :answer-field/id #uuid "d81b906d-dd91-53ff-b380-201beddb23f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268712}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80f02fdc-4719-513c-acae-f5bb8c001236" :question/math-academy-id "q-42433" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the coordinates of the maximum point of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962979/assets/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $\\pi $ and it is reached at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the maximum point of the function are $(-1,\\pi ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b0fd68b-2206-5d60-9ed8-d020751c3e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109824} {:answer/id #uuid "a82e4653-fbbf-5d31-bff0-08f83d38ba2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109825} {:answer/id #uuid "278b7fd9-fea1-53da-adca-6737f493d980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109826} {:answer/id #uuid "510cf5d7-65aa-55ad-8574-1f08d591f1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109827} {:answer/id #uuid "527e8da4-0626-5015-926f-8cdd9dacc30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "527e8da4-0626-5015-926f-8cdd9dacc30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109828} :answer-field/id #uuid "dae94f28-5c2c-538c-9ec1-c2044c4f7886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109823}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c5ae160-d78c-57fc-8666-ed9ed6778702" :question/math-academy-id "q-65828" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arccos x$?\n\n1. $(\\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{1}{2})$\n2. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{7π}{4})$\n3. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{3π}{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00a8fb88-4b07-57be-94c5-a6b499c9df5b" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac27788e-d3e9-5cd1-9751-e3ba1fa400a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962979/assets/3b4a2d419da9d35d9c69ec31893c91e3b4cb043d29fa16d41fd24992b5180fa7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268720} {:answer/id #uuid "867a7542-b138-5173-8fa7-6466071c8834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962979/assets/ad18edfcc765683e938c7c3fdc2dd91d188daf258201373cde21aef09429a058.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268721} {:answer/id #uuid "d261215f-758c-54a9-9aba-215f10a8dc19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962979/assets/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268722} {:answer/id #uuid "19779ffe-285c-554a-8cd9-5366f239e61f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962979/assets/d445a4870768a33f8da5cc2bd2e35013d076a890ff